13:25:03 | INFO | 🤖 Target: You're right to push back—and this is actually a great example of where the peripheral oxygen sensors *do* take charge. ## What's Happening at Altitude At high altitude, barometric pressure drops, so even though the percentage of oxygen in air stays ~21%, the partial pressure of oxygen is lower. This directly stimulates your **peripheral chemoreceptors** (carotid bodies), which sense oxygen levels in your arterial blood. Here's the key mechanism, called the **hypoxic ventilatory response**: - Low blood oxygen (hypoxia) directly activates carotid body chemoreceptors - This signal travels to your brainstem and triggers increased breathing rate/depth - This happens **independent of CO2 levels**—in fact, it happens despite what CO2 is doing ## The Interesting Twist When you hyperventilate in response to low oxygen at altitude, you actually blow off *more* CO2 than normal, dropping your CO2 levels below baseline (called hypocapnia). Normally, low CO2 would signal your body to *slow down* breathing. But at altitude, the oxygen-sensing drive **overrides** the CO2 system—your body prioritizes getting oxygen even at the cost of disrupting normal CO2/pH balance. This is part of why altitude sickness happens: the resulting respiratory alkalosis (blood becomes too alkaline from low CO2) contributes to symptoms like headache and nausea. Over several days at altitude, your kidneys compensate by excreting bicarbonate to help normalize blood pH—this is a key part of acclimatization. ## Bottom Line Both systems are real and work together: - **CO2/pH sensing** = the dominant, fine-tuned regulator during normal breathing at sea level - **Oxygen sensing** = kicks in strongly during significant hypoxia (altitude, lung disease, etc.) and can override the CO2 system So your mountain experience is a legitimate, direct demonstration of oxygen sensing in action—not just a side effect of CO2 changes.
